Routine Antenatal Shared Care Consultation
General Practice Other
Scheduled antenatal visit, Measurement of fundal height, Auscultation of fetal heart, Absence of red-flag symptoms

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-036
Reason for Visit: Acute maternal panic and extreme anxiety regarding perceived decreased fetal movements, requiring immediate clinical reassurance to prevent an emergency obstetric presentation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a 28-week pregnant female who experienced a sudden, severe panic attack after perceiving an absence of fetal movements over a two-hour period tonight. Despite having a scheduled routine visit upcoming, the acute psychiatric distress and the potential obstetric red flag required urgent face-to-face assessment to auscultate the fetal heart rate, rule out fetal demise, and provide immediate clinical stabilization. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period would have resulted in an unnecessary, highly stressful midnight presentation to the tertiary obstetric emergency department.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "I haven't felt the baby kick in two hours, I am absolutely terrified something has gone wrong, and I can't stop crying."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents as a pregnant female initially intending to attend a scheduled routine visit next week, but currently in a state of acute situational panic. An immediate assessment of maternal well-being confirms severe anxiety but an absolute absence of red-flag symptoms such as vaginal bleeding, fluid leakage, or severe abdominal pain. The patient reports normal, active fetal movements in the second and third trimesters until this evening, when she became hyper-focused on a quiet period. There is no history of pregnancy-induced hypertension or gestational diabetes.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently exhibiting pacing and severe psychomotor agitation.
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute maternal anxiety; partner is present and highly supportive.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 125/75 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Tearful, anxious, lying rigidly on the couch awaiting examination.
Physical Exam: Obstetric: Palpation of the abdomen to assess fundal height corresponding to gestational age reveals a symphysis-fundal height of 28cm, appropriate for dates. The abdomen is soft and non-tender with no uterine irritability. Auscultation of the fetal heart rate using a hand-held Doppler device confirms a strong, regular fetal heart rate of 145 bpm, with spontaneous accelerations noted during the brief examination. Measurement of maternal blood pressure and weight confirms baseline stability. Urinalysis to screen for proteinuria or asymptomatic bacteriuria is negative. Expected investigation results include normal physiological parameters and appropriate fetal growth on routine obstetric ultrasound tracking along established centiles.
Environmental Observations: Safe, quiet environment; patient has a cold glass of water prepared.
Medication Reconciliation: Prenatal vitamins and Folic Acid verified.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Routine Antenatal Shared Care Consultation (Secondary to Acute Maternal Anxiety) - Status: Stable (Fetal well-being confirmed)
Differentials:

Intrauterine Fetal Demise - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Immediate, clear auscultation of a robust fetal heart rate in the normal range (110-160 bpm) with audible fetal movement on the Doppler.

Placental Abruption - Ruled out/unlikely due to: A completely soft, non-tender uterus, absence of vaginal bleeding, and stable maternal vital signs.

Pre-eclampsia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normotensive blood pressure, absence of proteinuria on dipstick, and lack of headache or visual disturbances.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient experienced a severe acute stress reaction driven by a perceived reduction in fetal movements, a classic obstetric presentation that warrants immediate investigation. Telephone triage correctly identified the dual need to rule out fetal compromise and manage the acute maternal panic. Urgent face-to-face assessment was necessary to perform an immediate Doppler auscultation. The instantaneous, audible confirmation of the fetal heart rate provided absolute clinical reassurance, instantly arresting the panic attack and safely diverting the patient away from an overloaded emergency obstetric unit tonight. Deferring this assessment would have subjected the patient to unmanageable psychological trauma.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Performed immediate Doppler auscultation. Conducted a thorough discussion of upcoming routine antenatal screening tests to normalize the pregnancy trajectory. Provided specific dietary and lifestyle advice, including the "kick count" methodology and the effect of maternal position on perceived movement.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage future anxiety regarding fetal movements, commit to performing a structured "kick count" exactly once a day at 8:00 PM, lying quietly on your left side after a cold drink. Keep a dedicated notebook on your nightstand to record the time it takes to feel 10 movements. By structuring this check to a specific time and physical location, you actively prevent obsessive, continuous monitoring throughout the day, reinforcing a calm, deliberate assessment of fetal well-being.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine antenatal shared care appointment with the regular GP in 7 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call the maternity ward directly or present to the Obstetric Emergency Department if you ever feel fewer than 10 movements in a 2-hour period while lying on your side, if you experience any vaginal bleeding, or if your water breaks.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The baby's heart rate is perfect, and your panic has subsided. It is completely normal for babies to have sleep cycles where movements are less noticeable.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Always trust your instincts; if you feel a true, prolonged decrease in movement, seek medical help immediately, day or night.
PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: Your baby was simply in a sleep cycle, and your severe anxiety made it harder to notice subtle movements. The Doppler proves the baby is perfectly healthy and active right now.
Actionable Steps: Drink a cold glass of iced water tonight. Start your structured kick count notebook tomorrow evening. Attend your scheduled appointment next week.

